	<key>readme</key>
	<string>Type `hn` followed by anything to search your Hermes blocks. Results come from Talaria's local mirror, so this works with no network at all.

Alfred cannot see these through its own search: its default results are files, apps and contacts from the macOS metadata index, and CoreSpotlight app content is a separate store that nothing can inject into. So this workflow asks the daemon directly.

Return opens the block in the web app. Cmd-Return opens it via talaria://, which resolves the host at click time.

A Fallback Search is included: add it under Alfred Preferences &gt; Features &gt; Default Results &gt; Setup fallback results, and 'Search Hermes for ...' will appear whenever nothing on the Mac matches what you typed.

Type `hl` instead of `hn` to paste a *link* to a block into whatever you were writing in — the reciprocal of capture. Return pastes it; Cmd-Return pastes the other address (https vs talaria://); Alt-Return pastes just the title.

The link's shape is chosen for the destination: markdown where markdown is authored, a bare URL where it is not — because '[Title](https://...)' pasted into an email is literal brackets, and a bare URL autolinks there anyway. Alfred cannot tell the CLI which application you were in, since by then Alfred is the frontmost one, so `hl` takes the default. A hotkey that captures the frontmost app first can pass `--for &lt;bundle-id&gt;` and get the right shape every time.

Needs the talaria daemon running: `talaria doctor` will say.</string>
